{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-332461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda =20$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.05$\n- $Δx=0.12$\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-332461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =20,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <20Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{20} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.05$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.12$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=0.25$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality.\n\nHence, Euler's method is unstable for the step sizes $Δx=0.12,$ $Δx=0.2,$ and $Δx=0.25.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-332461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91950ec0b3d32ff97fbf30cc90da5a0e3ef0782031bb45c0b3fe32cc08880f20"} {"question" "q-332461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-332461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "II, III, and IV only"] ["text" "III and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-332461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II, III, and IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-333578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-333578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-333578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91950ec0b3d32ff97fbf30cc90da5a0e3ef0782031bb45c0b3fe32cc08880f20"} {"question" "q-333578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-333578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-333578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.8$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.7$\n- $\\lambda Δx=6.6$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.2)| & =|1-0.2+\\frac{(0.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{12\\,281}{15\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.818…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.8)| & =|1-1.8+\\frac{(1.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1427}{5000}| \\\\  & =0.2854<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.7)| & =|1-2.7+\\frac{(2.7)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.7)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.7)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{70\\,307}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.878…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.2)| & =|1-6.6+\\frac{(6.6)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(6.6)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(6.6)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{236\\,627}{5000}| \\\\  & =47.3254>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91950ec0b3d32ff97fbf30cc90da5a0e3ef0782031bb45c0b3fe32cc08880f20"} {"question" "q-334435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the trapezoidal method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.15$\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n- $\\lambda Δx=6.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the trapezoidal method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\text{ }\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx)\\,{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the trapezoidal method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the trapezoidal method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.15)| & =|\\frac{1-0.15/2}{1+0.15/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.925}{1.075}| \\\\  & =0.8604…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1-0.8/2}{1+0.8/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.6}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.4285…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|\\frac{1-2.9/2}{1+2.9/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.45}{2.45}| \\\\  & =0.1836…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6.2)| & =|\\frac{1-6.2/2}{1+6.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-2.1}{4.1}| \\\\  & =0.5121…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91950ec0b3d32ff97fbf30cc90da5a0e3ef0782031bb45c0b3fe32cc08880f20"} {"question" "q-334466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, III, and IV"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =4$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.45$\n- $Δx=0.9$\n- $Δx=2.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =4,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.45$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.9$ doesn't satisfy the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=2.2$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.2$ and $Δx=0.45.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "91950ec0b3d32ff97fbf30cc90da5a0e3ef0782031bb45c0b3fe32cc08880f20"} {"question" "q-334627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"} {"question" "q-334627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062923/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2f115fcd525c5410ef94fb279c5ec6ede052da9ae44639800a6e1fc5ded253"}] "usage" {"q-332461" []} "basis" 2052 "retractions" [[17592186229587 :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $λ = 20$ is the parameter of the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ > 0$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. $Δx = 0.05$\n2.